<% content_for :title, "Composition — Shadwire" %>
<% content_for :description, "How Shadwire components compose: nested helpers instead of props, items inside their group, titles on overlays, and when to render the class directly." %>
<% content_for :head do %>
  <%= tag.style rouge_stylesheet %>
<% end %>

<article class="space-y-12">
  <%= render "docs/header",
        title: "Composition",
        description: "You build a component by nesting helpers, the way shadcn/ui nests React components. The view ends up shaped like the HTML it renders." %>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("Helpers exist only for what is installed") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Each component installs its own helper module:
      <%= docs_code("button") %> writes <%= docs_code("app/helpers/ui/button_helper.rb") %>,
      which defines <%= docs_code("Ui::ButtonHelper#ui_button") %>. Rails automatically includes
      everything under <%= docs_code("app/helpers/") %>, so an installed helper is available in
      every view with no <%= docs_code("include") %>.
    </p>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      The helper of a component that is <em>not</em> installed does not exist, and calling it
      raises <%= docs_code("NoMethodError") %> when the page renders. Check
      <%= docs_code("installed[].helpers") %> before writing the view.
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block("bin/shadwire status --json\nbin/shadwire add carousel --yes", language: :shell) %>

    <%= render layout: "docs/callout", locals: { icon: "info", title: "A leftover ui_helper.rb at the root?" } do %>
      Older versions installed a single <code>app/helpers/ui_helper.rb</code>, which defines
      helpers for components you may not have. <code>status --json</code> flags it as
      <code>helpers.legacyHelperPresent</code>, and you can delete it.
    <% end %>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("Subcomponents are nested helpers") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      These components don't use ViewComponent slots, and they don't take their content as
      arguments. You put them together by nesting calls.
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:wrong_props]) %>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:right_props]) %>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      <%= docs_code("bin/shadwire info <name>") %> lists the helper for each part. Look it up
      rather than guessing: the helper is <%= docs_code("ui_card_content") %>, not
      <%= docs_code("ui_card_body") %>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("Use the whole composition") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Don't put everything into one part. The header, title and description each render their
      own markup, and putting all of it in the content loses that.
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:wrong_collapse]) %>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("Items go inside their group") %>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:wrong_group]) %>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:right_group]) %>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      The same goes for <%= docs_code("ui_tabs_trigger") %> inside
      <%= docs_code("ui_tabs_list") %>, <%= docs_code("ui_breadcrumb_item") %> inside
      <%= docs_code("ui_breadcrumb_list") %>, and <%= docs_code("ui_pagination_item") %> inside
      <%= docs_code("ui_pagination_content") %>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("Overlays always need a title") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      <%= docs_code("dialog") %>, <%= docs_code("alert-dialog") %>,
      <%= docs_code("sheet") %> and <%= docs_code("drawer") %> need their title component so
      screen readers can announce them, even when the design has no visible title.
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:wrong_title]) %>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:right_title]) %>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("Interactive components need Stimulus") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      <%= registry_stimulus_component_count %> of the <%= docs_component_items.size %> components ship a controller.
      <%= docs_code("bin/shadwire info <name>") %> tells you which through
      <%= docs_code("requiresStimulus") %>, and <%= docs_code("status.stack") %> says whether the
      app has importmap and stimulus-rails.
    </p>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      The controllers install into <%= docs_code("app/javascript/controllers/") %> and register
      themselves through <%= docs_code("controllers/index.js") %>'s
      <%= docs_code("eagerLoadControllersFrom(\"controllers\", application)") %>. If your app
      registers controllers explicitly, register the new ones there too.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("Rendering the class directly") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      The helper only wraps the class, so these two render the same thing:
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:helper_vs_class]) %>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      In views, use the helper. Use the class when you need the component object itself, or
      inside another ViewComponent. ViewComponents don't get helpers automatically, so include
      the module to call <%= docs_code("ui_button") %> from a component's template:
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:inside_component], language: :ruby) %>
  </section>
</article>
